    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the previous detour into the goblin metaphor. You are absolutely right to focus on the technical data for your production line. The uneven dye transfer on polyester is almost certainly a result of the specific combination of temperature, pressure, and time you mentioned.\n\nHere is the technical breakdown of why your current settings (200°C, 40s, 4 bar) are likely causing patchy results:\n\n1.  **Temperature Gradient:** Polyester requires a very specific temperature window (typically 180°C–195°C) to fully melt the polymer chains so the ink can bond. If your heat press has hot spots or uneven heating, the ink will cure on the hotter areas but fail to bond on the cooler spots, creating the \"patchy\" look you are seeing.\n2.  **Pressure Distribution:** 4 bar of pressure is generally standard, but if the platen isn't perfectly flat or if the fabric is unevenly draped, the pressure will be inconsistent. Areas with less pressure won't force the ink into the fiber pores effectively, leading to faded spots.\n3.  **Time vs. Heat:** 40 seconds might be sufficient if",
      "source": "target",
